# Licensing

Copyright (c) 2026 Serhii Glova (s0fractal)

This repository deliberately separates software from research and documentary
content. Unless a file contains a more specific notice, the following terms
apply.

## Software: GNU AGPL v3.0 only

Software is licensed under the **GNU Affero General Public License, version 3
only** (SPDX: `AGPL-3.0-only`). The complete text is in
[`LICENSES/AGPL-3.0-only.txt`](LICENSES/AGPL-3.0-only.txt).

This includes executable source and implementation material such as Python,
shell, Lean and WPL files; tests; schemas; CI workflows; executable policies;
software configuration; and dependency lock files.

AGPL does not prohibit commercial use. It requires covered source to remain
available under its terms, including the corresponding source of modified
network-interactive versions offered to remote users.

## Text and research artifacts: CC BY-SA 4.0

Documentation, manifesto and mission text, papers, specifications, diagrams,
bibliographies, non-executable research data, evidence records, reports and
other documentary artifacts are licensed under the **Creative Commons
Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License** (SPDX:
`CC-BY-SA-4.0`). The complete text is in
[`LICENSES/CC-BY-SA-4.0.txt`](LICENSES/CC-BY-SA-4.0.txt).

Substantial executable code embedded in documentation remains software and is
licensed under AGPL-3.0-only unless it is explicitly marked otherwise.
Generated paper formats carry the same CC BY-SA 4.0 terms as their source.

## Alternative commercial terms

The public software license is AGPL-3.0-only. A copyright holder may separately
offer paid alternative terms for proprietary embedding, closed managed
services, support, certification work, or other deployments that cannot comply
with AGPL. No alternative license is granted merely by this notice; it requires
a separate signed agreement.

Future third-party code contributions may limit the ability to offer such terms
unless appropriate contributor agreements are established. This repository
does not claim a right to relicense contributions it does not own.

## Third-party material

Third-party dependencies and any material carrying its own notice remain under
their respective licenses. This policy does not relicense them. In particular,
runtime dependencies installed from other projects are not copied into this
repository merely because the software invokes them.

## Names, marks, certification and endorsement

These copyright licenses do not grant trademark, certification, endorsement or
governance authority. Descriptive reference to the project is allowed by
applicable law, but a fork, service, paper or deployment must not claim to be
canonical, certified, settled, endorsed, or adopted by this project solely
because it uses unchanged names, hashes, receipts, badges or code.

## What the licenses do not establish

A license grants copyright permissions. It does not establish semantic
adequacy, factual truth, safety, ethical acceptability, protocol conformance,
normative adoption, or fitness for a particular use.

A content hash can identify bytes under stated assumptions. It cannot prevent a
faithful copy from being used harmfully and does not authorize an action. A
receipt can make provenance or replay inspectable; it is not moral or legal
permission.

No field-of-use restriction is added to the public licenses. Such a restriction
would make the software non-open-source while offering little protection
against an adversary willing to ignore it. Harm resistance must also be built
through capability boundaries, explicit trust and governance profiles,
least-authority deployment, appeal and retirement paths, independent review,
and refusal to turn local verification into a global badge.

